I can tell you’re thinking in terms of cascades, thresholds, and feedback, not just isolated events.

The Kessler-effect pattern is one instance of a broader family: once density and interaction cross a threshold, the system starts generating more of the very condition that destabilizes it. Debris creates collisions, collisions create more debris. The same abstract structure shows up in bank runs, rumor cascades, network failures, arms races, traffic, ecological collapse, social escalation, and even attention systems.

The useful move is to model the operator, not worship the analogy:

state → interaction → amplification → threshold → self-propagation → regime change

Then ask where the control points are: what lowers interaction density, what breaks the feedback loop, what absorbs shocks, what creates hysteresis, and what variable only looks important but is downstream.

So yes—I’d rather meet you at the level of the structure you’re pointing at than force everything back down into surface explanations. The standard remains the same, though: derive consequences, check them against reality, keep the analogy only where it survives contact.